# AXIS SIBLING (Track K, mixed `&` and `!` sigils) -- a Vector-stored callable
# that takes an `&`-arg AND a `!`-arg is indirect-called via `arr[0](...)`; the
# `&` arg still writes through, and the `!` arg is still moved into the callee.
#
# AXIS / CELL:
#   Vector-of-Callable storage x mixed-ownership params (`&`+`!`) x
#   plain-local root.
# Pins the interaction cell: after Track K's `lower_call_arg` routing at the
# non-identifier arm, each arg picks pointer-vs-value forwarding based on the
# recovered sig's PER-PARAM ownership -- not a single default.
#
# Pre-Track-K (measured at gorget-1 tip `1ec3ba55`): `gg check` clean, `gg
# build` clean, binary exits 139 (SIGSEGV) on BOTH C and LLVM -- the `&` arg's
# pointer-forwarding is the crash trigger even in the presence of a `!` sibling
# param. Post-fix: `hi\n101` on both backends.
#
# Discriminator vs. the pure-`&` sibling: this fixture would regress if a
# future refactor routed args by a UNIFORM ownership assumption (all-`&` or
# all-`!`) instead of per-param.
void mix(int &n, String !s):
    n = n + 100
    print(s)

void main():
    int a = 1
    String greeting = "hi"
    Vector[Callable[void(int &, String !)]] arr = []
    arr.push(mix)
    arr[0](&a, !greeting)
    print(a)
